**High beam** (*Fernlicht*) is your best friend on an empty country road and a menace nearly everywhere else. You must dip in good time:

- when a vehicle is **coming towards you**;
- when you are **following** another vehicle at close distance, because your beam bounces off their mirrors straight into their eyes;
- whenever safety otherwise requires it: a pedestrian, a cyclist, a horse, a deer;
- and on roads with **continuous, adequate lighting**.

Read that last one carefully, because the exam does. The ban is not "inside built-up areas". It is *on streets with continuous, adequate lighting*. A brightly lit stretch of Frankfurter Allee: no high beam. An unlit lane in an outer district, still comfortably inside the town sign: high beam is fine until somebody appears.

The law adds a duty most learners forget. When you dip, your view gets much shorter, so **take speed off as well**, "correspondingly slower" in the words of the rule. Dipping without slowing is how drivers arrive at parked lorries.

The headlight flash (*Lichthupe*) exists to warn, or to announce an intention to overtake outside built-up areas. Used to bully the car ahead out of the left lane it is intimidation, not communication.

Above a certain speed a tyre can no longer pump the water out of its grooves, so it climbs onto a wedge of water and stops touching the road at all. That is **aquaplaning**, and it does not announce itself with a bang. It announces itself like this:

- the steering suddenly goes **light, vague and spongy**, with no feedback at all;
- the engine note **rises** as the driven wheels spin up;
- a loud slapping, splashing noise from the wheel arches.

The correct reaction is deliberately boring: **ease off the accelerator, hold the wheel straight, do nothing sudden.** No stab at the brake, no corrective steering. The front tyres are not connected to the road, so every input you make is stored up and delivered all at once the instant grip returns, usually pointing you at the kerb. Declutch (or select N in an automatic) if you need to lose speed without locking anything, keep the wheel steady, and ride out the second or two.

Prevention beats recovery, because ABS and ESP cannot invent grip that the surface does not offer. Standing water in worn ruts, the shiny sheet across a dip after a Berlin thunderstorm, spray thrown up by a lorry: all of it is a reason to lift off *before* you get there. Your tread is the water pump. The **legal minimum is 1.6 mm**, but a tyre worn to 1.6 mm is a summer tyre in name only, and wet stopping distances grow dramatically as the grooves disappear.

**Wildlife** (*Wild*) moves at dawn and dusk, above all in spring and autumn, along the forest edges beyond the city. If an animal is on the road:

- **brake hard, keep both hands firmly on the wheel and stay in your lane**;
- **switch the high beam off**, because bright light transfixes an animal and it will stand there rather than leave;
- sound the horn to move it along;
- expect company. Deer travel in groups, so an empty road after the first one proves nothing.

Do **not swerve**. Swerving is how a repairable bonnet becomes a head-on collision or a tree, and you can only brake as hard as the traffic behind you allows.

If you do hit an animal: hazard lights, high-vis vest, warning triangle, then call the police, who will contact the local hunting tenant (*Jagdpächter*). Do not handle an injured animal, because a frightened deer is genuinely dangerous, and never take the carcass away with you; that counts as poaching (*Wilderei*).

**Glare** cuts both ways. Low winter sun straight down a long avenue like Karl-Marx-Allee, multiplied by a dirty windscreen, can erase a pedestrian completely. You are legally responsible for keeping your view clear, which makes clean glass, working wipers and a full washer bottle safety equipment rather than housekeeping. And when an oncoming driver forgets to dip, do not stare into the light and never retaliate with your own high beam: **look towards the right-hand edge of your own lane, slow down, hold your line** until they have passed, and stop somewhere safe if you truly cannot see.

Underneath all of it sits one habit. Keep your eyes moving, look where the road *will* be rather than at your own bonnet, and keep asking what could emerge from the gap you cannot see into. The general danger sign (*Gefahrstelle*) is the road authority admitting the same thing: something here deserves your attention, so arrive slower and ready to brake. Adjust your speed for what you cannot see, not for what you can.
